{
  "raw": {
    "https://uic.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/21104?c=uic": {
      "title": "Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Faculty - DrPH Director - Health Policy and Administration",
      "description": "Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Faculty - DrPH Director - Health Policy and Administration | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) English (UK) Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Faculty - DrPH Director - Health Policy and Administration Chicago, IL, United States | 1043301 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 - Application Deadline: 1/8/2027 Back to Search Open Rank Non-Tenure Track Faculty - DrPH Director - Health Policy and Administration Hiring Department : Health Policy and Administration Location :  Chicago, IL USA Requisition ID : 1043301 Salary: The salary range of employees currently holding equivalent positions is $95,000 - $175,000 per academic (9-month) year. POSITION SUMMARY The Division of Health Policy and Administration (HPA) at the University of Illinois Chicago (UIC) School of Public Health (SPH) seeks applications for a DrPH Program Director at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Clinical Professor, with a start date in Fall 2027. We are looking for a visionary leader committed to advancing the program's academic excellence, growth, and public health impact. This position is a full-time\ntenure track position intended to be eligible for benefits . This includes Health, Dental, Vision, Life\nInsurance, a Retirement Plan, Paid Time Off, and Tuition Waivers for employees\nand dependents. About the DrPH Program, HPA\nDivision, SPH, and UIC: The Doctor of Public Health\n(DrPH) in Leadership program explicitly focuses on strengthening\npractitioner-student knowledge, skills, and ability to understand and address\nthe persistent root causes of inequity. We emphasize whole-person,\ntransformational, and action- learning centered pedagogical approaches through\na cohort-based collective learning approach. The curriculum integrates and\nassures achievement of learning aligned with the Council on Education in Public\nHealth (CEPH) and UIC DrPH program competencies grounded in transformational\nleadership. Our courses are delivered in a synchronous distance-based model\nthat is supported by an ever-growing global community of student-practitioners,\nfaculty, alumni, staff, and strategic partners. The\nDivision of Health Policy and Administration (HPA) at the UIC SPH is an interprofessional, transdisciplinary\ncommunity addressing the underlying structural and social drivers of population\nhealth and disparities. Through mutually reinforcing teaching, research, and\npractice, we analyze, identify, and implement evidence-based solutions across\npolicy, systems, and organizations in Chicago, nationally, and globally.\nAnchored in community and cross-sector partnerships, our work responds to lived\nexperiences and real-world environments to achieve lasting impact. Our\nexpertise—centered on health disparities and equity—spans policy, law,\neconomics, health services, healthcare administration, and leadership. The UIC School of Public Health is the only Council on Education for Public Health (CEPH) accredited\nschool of public health in Illinois. UIC SPH is dedicated to enhancing quality\nof life by improving public health and plays a vital role in educating the\nnation’s public health workforce. UIC SPH consistently ranks among the top 10%\nof all schools and programs of public health nationally (ranked 22nd nationally\nin the 2026 U.S. News & World Report). For more than 50 years, UIC SPH has\nbeen the state's principal educator of public health professionals as well as researchers,\nacademic faculty, policymakers, and activists whose work positively affects\nhealth and well-being in communities that are diverse with respect to race,\nethnicity, socioeconomic status, gender, sexual orientation, nationality, and\nreligion. UIC SPH is one of seven health science colleges that comprise UI\nHealth, the only academic public health center in Chicago. UIC is\namong the nation’s preeminent urban public research universities, a Carnegie\nRU/VH research institution, and the largest university in Chicago. UIC serves\nover 37,000 students, comprising one of the most diverse student bodies in the\nnation and is designated as a Minority Serving Institution (MSI), an Asian\nAmerican and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPSI) and\na Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI). Through its 16 colleges, UIC produces\nnationally and internationally recognized multidisciplinary academic programs\nin concert with civic, corporate and community partners worldwide, including a\nfull complement of health sciences colleges. In 2026, UIC was awarded nearly\n$500 million in sponsored research funding, including $208 million from NIH. By\nemphasizing cutting-edge and transformational research along with a commitment\nto the success of all students, UIC embodies the dynamic, vibrant and engaged\nurban university. UIC is ranked #1 in the state of Illinois for social mobility\nby the Wall Street Journal and top 10 for social mobility nationally according\nto the U.S. News & WorldReport. UIC has 366,000 alumni and is one of the largest employers in\nthe city of Chicago. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS The successful candidate will hold a terminal degree, preferably a DrPH, and will have a distinguished record in public health leadership, teaching, and/or research, ideally reflecting a commitment to practice-based scholarship. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Preference will be given to candidates with experience teaching and advising doctoral-level students and with administrative leadership experience in academic or public health settings. APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS Applications received by December 1, 2026\nwill receive full consideration; the search will close on January 8, 2027. The anticipated start\ndate is August 2027. A completed online application that includes the following\nattachments is required for consideration: 1.\nA cover letter that includes a vision for the DrPH program, an overview of\nrelevant experience, and an indication of which position is preferred (Assistant,\nAssociate or Full). 2.\nA curriculum vitae (CV). 3.\nFor those applying at the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor, a recent\nscholarly writing sample (e.g., dissertation chapter or published peer-reviewed\narticle on which you are the sole or primary author). 4.\nOne-page teaching statement specifying training in pedagogy, teaching\nexperience, and potential courses within the school's DrPH curriculum. 5. Three letters of recommendation (for those\napplying at the assistant professor level) OR contact information for three\nprofessional references (for those applying at the associate or full professor\nlevel). For\nspecific questions about the application process, please contact HPA Business\nManager, Susan Lynch (slynch2@uic.edu). For questions regarding the position, please\ncontact the search committee chair, Dr. Yuka Asada (yasada2@uic.edu). The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. The university provides accommodations to applicants and employees. Request an Accommodation Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign University of Illinois Springfield University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, covid sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Select one: I am a current employee at UIC I am not a current employee at UIC I am a former employee at UIC Close Continue"
    },
    "https://uic.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/21090?c=uic": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Psychiatry",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Psychiatry | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) English (UK) Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Psychiatry Chicago, IL, United States | 1043179 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/12/2026 - Application Deadline: 11/15/2026 Back to Search Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Psychiatry Hiring Department : Department of Psychiatry Location :  Chicago, IL USA Requisition ID : 1043179 Salary: The previously determined salary range for this position was $220,000-$230,000 About the University of Illinois Chicago UIC is among the nation’s preeminent urban public research universities, a Carnegie RU/VH research institution, and the largest university in Chicago. UIC serves over 34,000 students, comprising one of the most diverse student bodies in the nation and is designated as a Minority Serving Institution (MSI), an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPSI) and a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI).  Through its 16 colleges, UIC produces nationally and internationally recognized multidisciplinary academic programs in concert with civic, corporate and community partners worldwide, including a full complement of health sciences colleges. By emphasizing cutting-edge and transformational research along with a commitment to the success of all students, UIC embodies the dynamic, vibrant and engaged urban university. Recent “Best Colleges” rankings published by U.S. News & World Report, found UIC climbed up in its rankings among top public schools in the nation and among all national universities. UIC has over 300,000 alumni, and is one of the largest employers in the city of Chicago. This position is intended to be eligible for benefits . This includes Health, Dental, Vision, Life Insurance, a Retirement Plan, Paid time Off, and Tuition waivers for employees and dependents. Position Summary: The Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Psychiatry will serve as a clinician-educator and provide patient care across inpatient and outpatient psychiatry services including specialty psychiatric programs while supporting the Department's academic and teaching mission. Responsibilities include supervising residents and fellows in the outpatient Neuropsychiatry Clinic, the Memory Clinic, and on the inpatient psychiatry Med-Psych team and/or the Consultation-Liaison service. The position also participates in the departmental physician call rotation, including overnight phone and weekend coverage. Duties & Responsibilities: Supervise residents and fellows in the outpatient Neuropsychiatry Clinic, the Memory Clinic, and on the inpatient psychiatry Med-Psych team and/or the Consultation-Liaison service. Provide clinical care across the Department's Psychosis, Mood/Anxiety, and Women’s Mental Health programs based on departmental need, and physician's interest and expertise. Serve as an on-call physician that requires some overnight (phone) and weekend on-call duties. Perform other related duties and participate in special projects as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Medical Degree. Completion of ACGME (Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education) accredited residency in Psychiatry. Completed fellowship training in Behavioral Neurology & Neuropsychiatry (Fellowship in Consultation-Liaison Psychiatry, Geriatric Psychiatry, and/or practice-track certification in Behavioral Neurology & Neuropsychiatry from the United Council for Neurologic Subspecialities will be considered). Candidate must possess or be eligible for an unrestricted Medical Licensure in the State of Illinois. Candidate must possess or be eligible for an unrestricted Controlled Substance license in the State of Illinois. Candidate must possess or be eligible for an unrestricted Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) license. Candidate must be Board-eligible or Board-certified in Psychiatry . The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. The university provides accommodations to applicants and employees. Request an Accommodation Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign University of Illinois Springfield University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, covid sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Select one: I am a current employee at UIC I am not a current employee at UIC I am a former employee at UIC Close Continue"
