    "https://uic.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/20734?c=uic": {
      "title": "Division Chief, Hematology/Oncology (Open Rank Tenure System Faculty) - Department of Medicine",
      "description": "Division Chief, Hematology/Oncology (Open Rank Tenure System Faculty) - Department of Medicine | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) English (UK) Division Chief, Hematology/Oncology (Open Rank Tenure System Faculty) - Department of Medicine Chicago, IL, United States | 1042665 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/2/2026 - Application Deadline: 11/2/2026 Back to Search SEARCH EXTENDED UNTIL NOVEMBER 2, 2026 Division Chief, Hematology/Oncology (Open Rank Tenure System Faculty) - Department of Medicine Hiring Department : Department of Medicine Location :  Chicago, IL USA Requisition ID : 1042665 Posting Close Date : October 15, 2026 Salary: The budgeted salary range for the position is $430,000 to $503,000. The pay offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors including (but not limited to) the experience and qualifications of the selected candidate including equivalent years in rank, training, and field or discipline; internal equity; and external market pay for comparable jobs. About the University of Illinois Chicago UIC is among the nation’s preeminent urban public research universities, a Carnegie RU/VH research institution, and the largest university in Chicago. UIC serves over 34,000 students, comprising one of the most diverse student bodies in the nation and is designated as a Minority Serving Institution (MSI), an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPSI) and a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI).  Through its 16 colleges, UIC produces nationally and internationally recognized multidisciplinary academic programs in concert with civic, corporate and community partners worldwide, including a full complement of health sciences colleges. By emphasizing cutting-edge and transformational research along with a commitment to the success of all students, UIC embodies the dynamic, vibrant and engaged urban university. Recent “Best Colleges” rankings published by U.S. News & World Report, found UIC climbed up in its rankings among top public schools in the nation and among all national universities. UIC has over 300,000 alumni, and is one of the largest employers in the city of Chicago. This position is intended to be eligible for benefits . This includes Health, Dental, Vision, Life Insurance, a Retirement Plan, Paid time Off, and Tuition waivers for employees and dependents. About\nthe Division of Hematology/Oncology The\nDivision of Hematology/Oncology at the University of Illinois Chicago is\ndedicated to advancing excellence in cancer care, benign hematology, research,\nand medical education. The Division provides comprehensive inpatient and\noutpatient services across the continuum of hematologic and oncologic care,\ndelivering innovative, patient-centered treatment to a diverse population\nthroughout Chicago and the surrounding region. The\nDivision is committed to improving outcomes through multidisciplinary clinical\nprograms, groundbreaking research, and the training of future leaders in\nhematology and oncology. Faculty members are actively engaged in\ninvestigator-initiated, cooperative group, translational, and clinical research\nprograms that seek to advance the understanding and treatment of cancer and\nblood disorders. The\nDivision features: Comprehensive hematology and oncology services,\n     including the diagnosis and treatment of benign and malignant hematologic\n     disorders, solid tumors, and complex cancer conditions. Multidisciplinary disease-focused programs that bring\n     together physicians, surgeons, radiation oncologists, researchers, nurses,\n     and support teams to provide coordinated patient care. Access to clinical trials and innovative therapies\n     through collaborations with UI Health, cooperative research groups, and\n     academic partners. A strong educational mission, including an\n     ACGME-accredited Hematology/Oncology Fellowship Program and extensive\n     teaching opportunities for medical students, residents, and fellows. Robust basic, translational, clinical, and population\n     health research programs focused on improving prevention, diagnosis,\n     treatment, and survivorship outcomes. A mission grounded in excellence across patient care,\n     scientific discovery, education, health equity, and community engagement. Position\nSummary: The\nDivision Chief will provide strategic, academic, clinical, and operational\nleadership for all activities within the Division of Hematology/Oncology,\nensuring alignment with the missions of the Department of Medicine, the\nUniversity of Illinois College of Medicine, and UI Health. The\nsuccessful candidate will be an accomplished physician-scientist,\nclinician-investigator, or academic physician with a national reputation in\nhematology and/or oncology. The Chief will be responsible for promoting\nclinical excellence, expanding research programs, advancing educational\ninitiatives, recruiting and developing faculty, and fostering a collaborative\nculture that supports innovation and academic achievement. Duties\n& Responsibilities: Leadership\n& Strategy Develop and implement a strategic vision that advances\n     the Division's clinical, research, and educational missions. Lead faculty recruitment, retention, mentorship, and\n     professional development initiatives. Promote a culture of collaboration, inclusion,\n     innovation, and accountability. Represent the Division within the Department of\n     Medicine, College of Medicine, UI Health, and external professional\n     organizations. Clinical\nExcellence Provide leadership for comprehensive hematology and\n     oncology clinical programs. Maintain an active clinical practice and promote\n     excellence in patient-centered care. Expand access to specialized hematologic and oncologic\n     services and identify opportunities for programmatic growth. Collaborate with multidisciplinary teams to enhance\n     quality, safety, and patient outcomes. Research\nAdvancement Maintain a nationally recognized record of scholarly\n     achievement and academic contributions. Foster growth in translational, clinical, population\n     health, and laboratory-based research programs. Support faculty in securing federal, foundation, and\n     industry-sponsored research funding. Promote interdisciplinary collaborations across the\n     University of Illinois Cancer Center and other institutional partners. Education\n& Training Support excellence in undergraduate, graduate, and\n     continuing medical education. Provide leadership and mentorship for fellows,\n     residents, and medical students. Partner with fellowship leadership to ensure compliance\n     with ACGME standards and maintain a robust learning environment. Encourage faculty engagement in teaching, mentoring,\n     and educational scholarship. Administrative\n& Financial Oversight Manage divisional finances, clinical operations,\n     workforce planning, and resource allocation. Ensure responsible stewardship of divisional resources\n     in support of strategic goals. Establish metrics and accountability measures to\n     evaluate divisional performance. Collaborate with Department, College, Cancer Center,\n     and Health System leadership to support institutional priorities. Minimum Qualifications: MD, DO, or equivalent degree. Board certification in Medical Oncology, Hematology, or Hematology and Medical Oncology. Qualification for appointment in the tenure system at the Associate Professor or Professor level within the University of Illinois College of Medicine. Demonstrated leadership experience within an academic medical center, cancer center, or healthcare organization. Distinguished record of clinical excellence, scholarship, and academic achievement. Evidence of successful mentoring, faculty development, and educational leadership. Demonstrated success in obtaining extramural funding and supporting research growth. Experience managing complex clinical, research, and educational programs. Eligible for a State of Illinois Medical License, State of Illinois Controlled Substance License, DEA License, and BLS Certification. Eligible for medical staff privileges at UI Health. The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. The university provides accommodations to applicants and employees. Request an Accommodation Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign University of Illinois Springfield University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, covid sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Select one: I am a current employee at UIC I am not a current employee at UIC I am a former employee at UIC Close Continue"
    },
    "https://uic.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/20709?c=uic": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Clinical Chemistry Laboratory/ Open Rank Faculty",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Clinical Chemistry Laboratory/ Open Rank Faculty | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) English (UK) Assistant Director of Clinical Chemistry Laboratory/ Open Rank Faculty Chicago, IL, United States | 1042442 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/31/2026 - Application Deadline: 10/30/2026 Back to Search Assistant Director of Clinical Chemistry Laboratory/ Open Rank Faculty Hiring Department : Pathology Location :  Chicago, IL USA Requisition ID : 1042442 Posting Close Date : For fullest\nconsideration, please submit a curriculum vitae and names of three\nreferences by October 30, 2026. Salary: The budgeted\nsalary range for the position is $120,000 to $250,000. Salary is competitive\nand commensurate with qualifications and experience, while also considering\ninternal equity. About the University of Illinois Chicago UIC is among the nation’s preeminent urban public research universities, a Carnegie RU/VH research institution, and the largest university in Chicago. UIC serves over 34,000 students, comprising one of the most diverse student bodies in the nation and is designated as a Minority Serving Institution (MSI), an Asian American and Native American Pacific Islander Serving Institution (AANAPSI) and a Hispanic Serving Institution (HSI).  Through its 16 colleges, UIC produces nationally and internationally recognized multidisciplinary academic programs in concert with civic, corporate and community partners worldwide, including a full complement of health sciences colleges. By emphasizing cutting-edge and transformational research along with a commitment to the success of all students, UIC embodies the dynamic, vibrant and engaged urban university. Recent “Best Colleges” rankings published by U.S. News & World Report, found UIC climbed up in its rankings among top public schools in the nation and among all national universities. UIC has over 300,000 alumni, and is one of the largest employers in the city of Chicago. This position is intended to be eligible for benefits . This includes Health, Dental, Vision, Life Insurance, a Retirement Plan, Paid time Off, and Tuition waivers for employees and dependents. Position\nSummary The Department of Pathology at the\nUniversity of Illinois at Chicago College of Medicine is seeking a full-time\nacademic Clinical Chemist to join our exceptional faculty and serve as\nAssistant Director of the Laboratory Section of Clinical Chemistry.  This is an open-rank faculty position. Rank\nand tenure will be commensurate with the candidate's experience,\nqualifications, and funding. The Department of Pathology includes the divisions of\nAnatomic Pathology, Transdisciplinary Pathology, Research, and Clinical\nPathology which includes Clinical Chemistry, Blood Banking/Transfusion\nMedicine, Hematopathology, HLA, Clinical Microbiology, and Molecular\nDiagnostics clinical laboratories. Faculty expertise supports strong clinical\nprograms at UI Health in solid organ transplant, bone marrow transplant,\nneurology, gastroenterology, hematology and oncology, obstetrics and\ngynecology, pediatrics and more. Opportunities exist to participate and\ncollaborate on clinical research.  The\nDepartment hosts an ACGME accredited Anatomic and Clinical Pathology residency\nprogram and several ACGME accredited fellowship programs. The UIC Department of\nPathology is among the country’s fastest-growing academic departments and is\nstructured to provide a unique environment to foster the development of\nacademic careers.  UIC is one of the\ncountry’s largest and most diverse medical schools and ranks among the top\ncolleges of medicine in the growth of extramural grant funding. Academic\nappointments necessitate participation in medical education and institutional,\nregional, and national conferences.  A\nhigh commitment to excellence in clinical service with outstanding\ncommunication skills is essential. In addition to providing medical education and diagnostic\nservices for Clinical Chemistry, the successful candidate will be expected to\nestablish and maintain a program in translational research at UIC. Fellowship training and significant experience in Clinical\nChemistry is required for this position. Preference will be given to candidates\nwith a track record of scholarly achievement. Duties &\nResponsibilities Provide diagnostic services for Clinical Chemistry Along with the Director of Clinical Chemistry: Ensure test systems provide quality service in all aspects of testing – preanalytic, analytic, and post-analytic Ensure selection of quality test systems relevant to accuracy, precision, verification, and method selection Develop and monitor quality measures with yearly plans to improve quality and efficiency Ensure enrollment, participation, and return of Proficiency Testing Ensure that quality control and quality assurance programs are established and maintained Ensure that remedial actions are taken and documented Ensure that an approved procedure, process and policy manuals are available and reviewed annually Ensure that interpretative tests and consultation reports include all pertinent information Assist in developing a working relationship with Clinical Service Units Participate in management of critical testing and critical test reporting and monitoring Provide medical education in Clinical Chemistry Provide education to medical students, residents, fellows, graduate students and post-doctoral fellows Monitor and assist colleagues and subordinates in the preparation and presentation of appropriate intramural and extramural lectures and conferences Participate in appropriate continue education programs such as in-service lectures for laboratory employees or medical staff Establish and maintain a program in translational research at UIC Actively participate in basic, translational and/or clinical research or investigative pursuits, including but not limited to, clinical informatics, clinical trials involving patients, new instrumentation or innovative testing methodologies, etc. Collaborate in and cooperate with ongoing Departmental or inter-departmental research projects in the College of Medicine, other UI Colleges of School and/or external academic institutions. Actively mentor and involve medical students, residents and fellows in research Perform other tasks as assigned by Clinical Pathology leadership to support departmental goals. Minimum\nQualifications Candidates for this position must have a PhD, MD or DO\ndegree. PhD degree holding candidates must possess board certification in\nClinical Chemistry from the American Board of Clinical Chemistry. MD or DO\ndegree holding candidates must be certified in Chemical Pathology by the\nAmerican Board of Pathology or in Clinical Chemistry by the American Board of\nClinical Chemistry. The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. The university provides accommodations to applicants and employees. Request an Accommodation Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign University of Illinois Springfield University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, covid sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. Select one: I am a current employee at UIC I am not a current employee at UIC I am a former employee at UIC Close Continue"
